{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And every swarming thing that swarms upon the earth is a detestable thing; it shall not be eaten.</b> These are those born in fruits — they are the worms in dates and dried figs, and the tiny creatures in lentils and beans and other fruits. And afterward it mentions 'every swarming thing that swarms upon the earth,' and these are those born from male and female, and this is the sense of 'swarms' in the language of (Genesis 9:7) ״שרצו בארץ ורבו בה״, (Exodus 1:7) ״פרו וישרצו״. And a person endangers himself by eating them — they are the snake, the earthworm, the scorpion, the spider, and the centipede. And so the rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'whatever goes on its belly' — this is the snake; 'whatever' (כל) includes the small earthworm; 'goes on four' — this is the scorpion; 'whatever' includes the spider and what resembles the spider; 'has many legs' — this is the centipede, which has many legs; 'whatever' includes what resembles it. And afterward it mentions 'in all the swarming thing that swarms upon the earth,' and these are those born from decay."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "המתילדים rendered as 'those born in/from' per Rabbeinu Bahya's usage for spontaneously generated creatures; מהעפוש rendered hyper-literally as 'from decay' — the word is textually obscure, possibly a variant spelling related to rot/decay, and the translation preserves the difficulty without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:55.972693673Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}